catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ 04 — generics, and what staging means.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D01 §3.2, D-02, D-83): a generic is not a second mechanism. It is
\ the SAME `specialize` that runs the Dictionary in demo 03, run at
\ elaboration time instead of at run time. So a generic call leaves no name,
\ no dictionary entry, no lookup and no type argument in the compiled program
\ — the code that is there is the code for the types you called it at, and
\ `locate` will show you.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- Declaring one. Type parameters go in `[…]` and appear as `#T`.
\ A generic MUST write its signature: inference never generalises, so there
\ would be nothing to generalise from.
catcat> define twice[#T] ( #T -- #T #T ) { dup }
generic twice[#T]
catcat> 5 twice show print " " print show print "\n" print
5 5
ok  (empty)
catcat> "hi" twice print print "\n" print
hihi
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- The types come from the stack, and each call gets its own copy, spliced
\ into the caller. `quad` is written once over `#T` and calls `twice` three
\ times; `q` calls it at `i64`. Nothing polymorphic survives:
catcat> define quad[#T] ( #T -- #T #T #T #T ) { twice twice twice }
generic quad[#T]
catcat> define q ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 ) { quad }
defined q ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 )
catcat> locate q
define q ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 ) {
  dup dup dup
}
catcat> \ That is the whole zero-cost story, and it is not a promise about an
\ optimizer. There is nowhere for a cost to hide: the residual is the program.
catcat> \ --- Generics call generics, at concrete types or at their own parameters.
catcat> define outer[#T] ( #T -- #T #T ) { twice[#T] }
generic outer[#T]
catcat> 9 outer show print " " print show print "\n" print
9 9
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ What one may NOT do is call itself, directly or through another. A call is
\ expanded where it stands, so a self-call asks to be expanded forever — and
\ this is caught by NAME, so it also catches mutual and polymorphic recursion.
catcat> define loopy[#T] ( #T -- #T ) { loopy[#T] }
generic loopy[#T]
catcat> 1 loopy
error: loopy is already being instantiated; a generic may not be recursive, directly or through another
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Writing the types out
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ Matched types have to be pinned down by the INPUTS — the outputs are what
\ the call is trying to work out. A generic whose parameter appears only in
\ the output must be instantiated explicitly.
catcat> define mk0[#T] ( -- #T ) { 0 }
generic mk0[#T]
catcat> mk0
error: mk0: #T is not determined by the inputs; write the types at the call site, as in mk0[i64]
catcat> mk0[i64] show print "\n" print
0
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Written types are CHECKED, not believed. They go to the same matcher, so the
\ stack must still agree and the body must still typecheck at them.
catcat> mk0[str]
error: mk0, instantiated: declares ( -- str ) but its body has ( -- i64 )
catcat> 1 twice[str]
error: twice: the stack does not match its declared inputs
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Linearity crosses generics with no extra rule
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ `twice` is `{ dup }`, and `dup` needs its type to be `Copy`. `Box[t]` is
\ neither `Copy` nor `Drop` — it is linear — so `twice` is simply not
\ available at that type. The error comes from the body, checked at the call.
catcat> define boxy ( Box[i64] -- Box[i64] Box[i64] ) { twice }
error: twice, instantiated: dup: this value's type is not Copy
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Generics compose with effects, and with the Dictionary
\ ======================================================================
catcat> effect Log { declare say ( str -- ) }
effect Log say
catcat> \ A generic body may perform operations. The row is part of the schema, so
\ every instance carries it.
catcat> define watched[#T] ( #T -- #T !Log ) { "  saw a value\n" say }
generic watched[#T]
catcat> define use ( i64 -- i64 !Log ) { watched 1 + }
defined use ( i64 -- i64 !Log )
catcat> "(a) a generic that logs:\n" print
handle Log over ( i64 ) init { 0 } { say { swap pop 1 + } } { 41 use }
show print " message(s), result " print show print "\n" print
(a) a generic that logs:
1 message(s), result 42
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- A LIMIT, and it is the price of the staging above. Demo 03's rebinding
\ does NOT reach into a generic instance. An instance is a `TSpecialize`, and
\ specializing resolves every word it calls right there — so by the time a
\ caller's `with` runs there is no call left to rebind:
catcat> define bump ( i64 -- i64 ) { 1 + }
define stepper[#T] ( #T i64 -- #T i64 ) { bump }
define big_bump ( i64 -- i64 ) { 1000 + }
defined bump ( i64 -- i64 )
generic stepper[#T]
defined big_bump ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> define run ( -- str i64 ) { "tag" 0 stepper }
define run_big ( -- str i64 ) { with { bump big_bump } { "tag" 0 stepper } }
defined run ( -- str i64 )
defined run_big ( -- str i64 )
catcat> run swap pop show print " " print
run_big swap pop show print "\n" print
1 1
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Both print 1. The residual says why — `bump` is already gone:
catcat> locate run
define run ( -- str i64 ) {
  "tag" 0 1 +
}
catcat> \ Rebinding a word a generic calls therefore has to happen before the
\ instantiation, not around it. Whether that is the right order is open, and
\ tracked as Q-21 in NOTES/N02_Open_Questions.md; it is the same question as
\ Q-20, which asks whether a generic body should close over the environment of
\ its declaration or read it at each use.
catcat> \ --- Sharing. Identical instantiations are ELABORATED once and the result
\ reused, which is what keeps nesting from multiplying out: this chain would
\ otherwise build 3^4 copies of `twice`, and it returns immediately.
catcat> define g2[#T] ( #T -- #T #T #T #T ) { quad[#T] pop pop pop quad[#T] pop pop pop quad[#T] }
define g3[#T] ( #T -- #T #T #T #T ) { g2[#T] pop pop pop g2[#T] pop pop pop g2[#T] }
generic g2[#T]
generic g3[#T]
catcat> define deep ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 ) { g3[i64] }
defined deep ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 )
catcat> locate deep
define deep ( i64 -- i64 i64 i64 i64 ) {
  dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup pop pop pop dup dup dup
}
catcat> \ What is not yet shared is the emitted CODE — each call site carries its own
\ copy of the residual. The copies are now structurally equal, so what is
\ missing is a common-subexpression pass over the core rather than anything
\ about generics. Tracked as Q-19 in NOTES/N02_Open_Questions.md.
